1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:02,600
Okay, moving on to nephrotic syndrome.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:04,640
These are glomerular disorders,

3
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:07,100
the hallmark of which is proteinuria,

4
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:09,540
and of course, that proteinuria needs to be greater

5
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:11,920
than 3.5 grams per day.

6
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:13,240
Now, the major protein in the blood

7
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:14,720
and the major protein that is lost

8
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:17,540
in this particular group of disorders is albumin,

9
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:20,260
and so the patients become hypoalbuminemic.

10
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:22,300
Now, of course, albumin plays an important role

11
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:24,540
in maintaining the oncotic pressure of the blood,

12
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:26,180
so a loss of albumin would result

13
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:27,940
in a decreased oncotic pressure,

14
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:30,380
and the decreased oncotic pressure would result

15
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:35,020
in increased fluid within the tissue space or edema,

16
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:39,000
and so the consequence of the hypoalbuminemia will be edema.

17
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,440
In addition, the patients will lose gamma globulin,

18
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:43,700
which is another important protein within the blood,

19
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:45,240
and the loss of gamma globulin

20
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:47,700
will result in hypogammal globulinemia,

21
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:50,980
which, of course, then will increase the risk of infection.

22
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:52,460
In addition, the patients will develop

23
00:00:52,460 --> 00:00:54,100
a hypercoagulable state.

24
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:56,780
Now, the reason that they develop a hypercoagulable state

25
00:00:56,780 --> 00:01:01,440
is that they preferentially lose antithrombin 3.

26
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:03,640
So if they lose antithrombin 3,

27
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:06,660
they've lost one of their anticoagulant proteins,

28
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:08,740
and so they become procoagulable.

29
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:11,060
Remember that antithrombin 3 plays the important role

30
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:12,460
of breaking up thrombin,

31
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:15,140
and also of destroying coagulation factors

32
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:16,820
so that you can't produce thrombin.

33
00:01:16,820 --> 00:01:19,460
So it is an anticoagulant protein,

34
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:22,700
and if you lose it, you're gonna get hypercoagulable.

35
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:25,220
And finally, the patients are going to have hyperlipidemia

36
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:26,800
and hypercholesterolemia.

37
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:28,000
Now, the way I think about this

38
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,820
is because there's loss of so much protein from the blood,

39
00:01:30,820 --> 00:01:33,520
the blood becomes, quote-unquote, relatively thin,

40
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:36,360
and so the liver reacts by throwing excess fat

41
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:38,900
into the blood to sort of beef it up a little bit.

42
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:41,480
And it's a funny way of thinking about it, but it works.

43
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:44,440
And so, therefore, the patients become hyperlipidemic

44
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:47,040
and hypercholesterolemic, and this is important.

45
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:49,900
Board examiners like to ask about this as well.

46
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:51,440
The first of the nephrotic syndromes

47
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:54,280
is minimal change disease, this is the most common cause

48
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,400
of nephrotic syndrome in children.

49
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:58,080
It's usually idiopathic.

50
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:00,400
That's not anything special because the vast majority

51
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:03,000
of nephrotic syndromes are usually idiopathic.

52
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,820
However, it can be associated with Hodgkin's lymphoma,

53
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:07,340
and that's super high yield.

54
00:02:07,340 --> 00:02:09,440
In order to understand this association,

55
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:11,280
let's go back and remind ourselves

56
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:15,180
that the glomerular filtration barrier contains three layers.

57
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:17,640
The first is the endothelial cell,

58
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:21,860
sitting on top of a basement membrane, upon which sits

59
00:02:21,860 --> 00:02:24,740
the podocytes, or I should say more particularly,

60
00:02:24,740 --> 00:02:27,520
the foot processes of the podocytes.

61
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:30,400
Of course, this is also called, this layer of foot processes

62
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,240
is also called the epithelial cells.

63
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:35,800
And so you can think of it as endothelial cells

64
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:37,700
sitting on top of a basement membrane,

65
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:40,640
upon which sit the epithelial cells,

66
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:43,520
or the foot processes of the podocytes.

67
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:46,820
Now, this tri-layer forms the filtration barrier

68
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:50,000
for the glomerulus into the tubular space.

69
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,480
In minimal change disease, you lose the foot processes.

70
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:55,780
So if this is the endothelial cells here,

71
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:57,860
you get effacement of the foot processes,

72
00:02:57,860 --> 00:02:59,280
with this being normal,

73
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:01,520
and then flattening of the foot processes here

74
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:03,060
being abnormal.

75
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:05,780
Now, what causes the effacement of the foot processes

76
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:09,460
in this particular disorder is the production of cytokines.

77
00:03:09,460 --> 00:03:14,820
And so cytokines mediate this particular damage

78
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:16,340
to the foot processes.

79
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:19,100
Now, recall that in patients with Hodgkin's lymphoma,

80
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:21,540
you get massive overproduction of cytokines

81
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:23,100
by the Reed-Sternberg cells.

82
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:25,380
In fact, that's why patients with Hodgkin's lymphoma

83
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:27,220
can often present with B symptoms,

84
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:29,520
because the cytokines get released into the blood,

85
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:32,480
giving you fever, chills, night sweats, et cetera.

86
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:35,300
And so in the same way, these cytokines can hit the kidney,

87
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:37,200
and when they hit the glomerular filtration barrier,

88
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:39,360
they can knock out the podocytes, resulting

89
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:41,100
in minimal change disease.

90
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:43,540
Now, regardless of the cause, whether it be idiopathic

91
00:03:43,540 --> 00:03:45,600
or associated with Hodgkin's lymphoma,

92
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:47,560
the key findings in minimal change disease

93
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:50,500
will be normal glomeruli on the H&E stain.

94
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:51,800
You might see a little bit of lipid

95
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:53,540
within the proximal tubular cell.

96
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:55,520
However, the hallmark of this disease

97
00:03:55,520 --> 00:03:58,220
is normal glomeruli on H&E stain.

98
00:03:58,220 --> 00:04:00,400
The key finding, however, is that on EM,

99
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:03,500
the patients will have effacement of the foot processes,

100
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:06,280
as I've already illustrated in our diagram.

101
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:08,340
Now, it's important to note that this disorder

102
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:11,480
is not driven by the deposition of immune complexes,

103
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:13,380
and so therefore, the immunofluorescence

104
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:14,520
will be negative.

105
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:17,020
The immunofluorescence is basically most useful

106
00:04:17,020 --> 00:04:19,020
when you have immune complex deposits,

107
00:04:19,020 --> 00:04:21,120
and so you won't really see anything specific

108
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:22,720
on the immunofluorescence.

109
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:25,280
Now, another important thing about minimal change disease

110
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:27,220
is that you get selective proteinuria.

111
00:04:27,220 --> 00:04:28,680
You do lose albumin, however,

112
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:30,960
you do not lose immunoglobulin.

113
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:33,160
Otherwise stated, the change is so minimal

114
00:04:33,160 --> 00:04:36,220
that the only protein that is lost is albumin.

115
00:04:36,220 --> 00:04:38,620
This has an excellent response to steroids,

116
00:04:38,620 --> 00:04:40,160
and again, that's because the damage

117
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:41,720
is mediated by cytokines.

118
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:44,400
So if you give steroids and you knock out the ability

119
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:46,760
to produce cytokines, that would then result,

120
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:49,020
of course, in an excellent response.

121
00:04:49,020 --> 00:04:50,720
And it's important to note that this is really

122
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:52,860
the only nephrotic syndrome in which you get

123
00:04:52,860 --> 00:04:54,900
an excellent response to treatment.

124
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:57,520
This is a histologic image of a glomerulus

125
00:04:57,520 --> 00:04:59,920
taken from a patient with minimal change disease.

126
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:02,380
Of course, in minimal change disease on H and E,

127
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:04,420
the glomerulus should look normal.

128
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:06,380
And this basically is a normal glomerulus,

129
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:07,980
but it gives us an opportunity to review

130
00:05:07,980 --> 00:05:09,880
some normal histology as well.

131
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:12,680
Now, recall that the outer capsule here

132
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:14,220
is called Bowman's capsule.

133
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:16,020
This is called Bowman's space,

134
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:17,720
and then you actually have the glomerulus

135
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:18,960
sitting here in the middle.

136
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:21,820
Now, the glomerulus is divided into lobules,

137
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:24,460
and that's the functional unit of the glomerulus,

138
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:26,520
so you've got all these lobules here.

139
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:30,160
And remember that the lobules are held together by mesangial cells,

140
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:33,220
and so this pink region here represents the mesangium.

141
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:36,080
And the mesangium holds together these capillary loops

142
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:39,060
upon which the filtration actually occurs.

143
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:41,720
And, of course, the filtration barrier

144
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:43,420
should be very thin and delicate.

145
00:05:43,420 --> 00:05:45,980
And notice that the wall here of the capillaries,

146
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:49,420
as I'm highlighting here, is very thin and delicate.

147
00:05:49,420 --> 00:05:51,680
And so this is a normal glomerulus.

148
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:54,680
Now, on EM, which is where you're going to find the key changes

149
00:05:54,680 --> 00:05:57,180
with minimal change disease, you're going to get effacement

150
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:59,640
of the foot processes, as I'm marking here.

151
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:01,020
Again, these are red blood cells,

152
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:03,140
so this would be the blood vessel.

153
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:05,380
Here is the endothelial cells here.

154
00:06:05,380 --> 00:06:06,940
This is the basement membrane.

155
00:06:06,940 --> 00:06:09,340
And then this is where the foot processes should be.

156
00:06:09,340 --> 00:06:11,740
However, you can clearly see that the foot processes

157
00:06:11,740 --> 00:06:14,080
are effaced or flattened.

158
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:17,140
And again, that's the hallmark of minimal change disease.

159
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:20,320
The next disorder is focal segmental glomerulus sclerosis.

160
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:22,880
This is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome

161
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:25,760
in Hispanics and African Americans.

162
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:27,720
Again, as with most nephrotic syndromes,

163
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:29,320
it's usually idiopathic.

164
00:06:29,320 --> 00:06:32,020
However, it can be associated with HIV,

165
00:06:32,020 --> 00:06:34,480
heroin use, or sickle cell disease.

166
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:36,560
This is very high yield.

167
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:38,680
Now, let me just remind you that if you have a patient

168
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:41,320
on an examination or in the clinics,

169
00:06:41,320 --> 00:06:45,260
and they have HIV and then develop nephrotic syndrome,

170
00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:47,020
the nephrotic syndrome that they have

171
00:06:47,020 --> 00:06:50,560
is focal segmental glomerulus sclerosis.

172
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:53,180
Similarly, the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome

173
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:55,220
in heroin users is going to be

174
00:06:55,220 --> 00:06:57,460
focal segmental glomerulus sclerosis.

175
00:06:57,460 --> 00:06:59,020
And if you have a sickle cell patient

176
00:06:59,020 --> 00:07:00,780
and they develop nephrotic syndrome,

177
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:01,960
again, you're gonna think about

178
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:05,220
focal segmental glomerulus sclerosis.

179
00:07:05,220 --> 00:07:07,860
Now, the name focal segmental glomerulus sclerosis

180
00:07:07,860 --> 00:07:09,960
actually gives away what we're going to see

181
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:11,620
in this particular disorder.

182
00:07:11,620 --> 00:07:13,820
The words glomerulus sclerosis imply

183
00:07:13,820 --> 00:07:16,060
that we're going to see a dense pink deposition

184
00:07:16,060 --> 00:07:18,420
of collagen within the glomerulus.

185
00:07:18,420 --> 00:07:21,420
In addition, this deposition will be segmental.

186
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:23,760
And what segmental means is that if you were to look

187
00:07:23,760 --> 00:07:25,520
at the circle of the glomerulus,

188
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:28,460
it would only involve one segment of the glomerulus,

189
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:31,700
whereas other segments of the glomerulus would be normal.

190
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:35,800
Finally, the word focal means that you will have involvement

191
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:38,720
of only some of the glomeruli within the kidney.

192
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:41,420
And so, for example, if there were three glomeruli

193
00:07:41,420 --> 00:07:44,160
within the kidney, maybe only one would be involved.

194
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:46,520
And, of course, there aren't three glomeruli in the kidney,

195
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:48,520
but I'm just simply using that as an example.

196
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:51,860
So, again, focal, one out of three, for example.

197
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:54,460
Segmental, the one that is involved,

198
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:56,860
only part of the globe or part of that circle

199
00:07:56,860 --> 00:07:58,020
is actually involved.

200
00:07:58,020 --> 00:07:59,520
And what's the lesion?

201
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:02,520
Pink sclerosis within the glomerulus on H and E.

202
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:05,880
Now, in this particular disorder, you get effacement

203
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:07,880
of the foot processes on EM.

204
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:10,080
Now, here's how I think about this.

205
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:12,380
What you need to remember is that when you have a patient

206
00:08:12,380 --> 00:08:14,820
with minimal change disease, the vast majority

207
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:16,880
of those patients respond to steroids.

208
00:08:16,880 --> 00:08:19,180
However, if you were to give a patient with minimal change

209
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:21,980
disease steroids and they didn't respond,

210
00:08:21,980 --> 00:08:24,660
what they would progress to would be focal segmental

211
00:08:24,660 --> 00:08:25,980
glomerulosclerosis.

212
00:08:25,980 --> 00:08:29,020
So, it's sort of the progression of what happens

213
00:08:29,020 --> 00:08:31,740
in minimal change disease were the disease

214
00:08:31,740 --> 00:08:33,480
to not respond to steroids.

215
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:35,520
And so that helps me to remember that you're going to see

216
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:38,580
the exact same pathology on EM,

217
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:41,220
which is that you'll see effacement of the foot processes.

218
00:08:41,220 --> 00:08:43,940
Now, in this disorder, it's not driven

219
00:08:43,940 --> 00:08:45,880
by immune complex deposition.

220
00:08:45,880 --> 00:08:47,340
And therefore, because it's not driven

221
00:08:47,340 --> 00:08:49,020
by immune complex deposition,

222
00:08:49,020 --> 00:08:51,180
you really won't see anything significant

223
00:08:51,180 --> 00:08:52,540
on immunofluorescence.

224
00:08:52,540 --> 00:08:54,480
You can see some nonspecific findings,

225
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:57,900
but for your purposes of grasping this material

226
00:08:57,900 --> 00:09:00,380
for examinations and a general understanding,

227
00:09:00,380 --> 00:09:02,400
you're not going to really see anything specific

228
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:04,180
on immunofluorescence.

229
00:09:04,180 --> 00:09:06,180
Okay, here's a picture of focal segmental

230
00:09:06,180 --> 00:09:07,600
glomerulosclerosis.

231
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:09,900
I've shown you the one involved glomerulus.

232
00:09:09,900 --> 00:09:12,240
And so here's the globe of the glomerulus.

233
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:14,300
You can clearly see that there's sclerosis

234
00:09:14,300 --> 00:09:15,480
within the glomerulus.

235
00:09:15,480 --> 00:09:17,000
However, it's segmental.

236
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:18,240
Here it's normal.

237
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:20,280
And here you have sclerosis.

238
00:09:20,280 --> 00:09:22,840
Now, of course, focal segmental glomerulosclerosis

239
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:25,240
is going to have a poor response to steroids.

240
00:09:25,240 --> 00:09:27,020
The vast majority of nephrotic syndromes,

241
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:28,480
except minimal change disease,

242
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:30,240
usually don't respond to treatment.

243
00:09:30,240 --> 00:09:33,420
And so it'll progress to chronic renal failure.

244
00:09:33,420 --> 00:09:34,820
The next of the nephrotic syndromes

245
00:09:34,820 --> 00:09:36,720
is membranous nephropathy.

246
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:38,120
Now, this is the most common cause

247
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:40,440
of nephrotic syndrome in Caucasian adults.

248
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:42,520
And that's particularly high yield.

249
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:43,980
It's usually idiopathic.

250
00:09:43,980 --> 00:09:45,720
But of course, that's nothing special

251
00:09:45,720 --> 00:09:48,440
because nephrotic syndromes are usually idiopathic.

252
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:51,500
However, it can be associated with hepatitis B or C,

253
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:54,540
solid tumors, lupus, or some drugs.

254
00:09:54,540 --> 00:09:57,000
Now lupus is particularly important here.

255
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,100
Let me just remind you that patients with lupus,

256
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:01,900
the most common cause of death is renal failure.

257
00:10:01,900 --> 00:10:05,680
And the most common disorder that they get within the kidney

258
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:07,640
is actually a nephritic syndrome

259
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:11,080
called diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis.

260
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:13,040
However, lupus patients can also present

261
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:14,480
with nephrotic syndrome.

262
00:10:14,480 --> 00:10:16,640
And if they present with nephrotic syndrome,

263
00:10:16,640 --> 00:10:18,980
then the nephrotic syndrome that they develop

264
00:10:18,980 --> 00:10:20,920
is membranous nephropathy.

265
00:10:20,920 --> 00:10:24,080
And that's very high yield for board examinations.

266
00:10:24,080 --> 00:10:26,240
So it is important to note these associations.

267
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:31,220
For example, hepatitis B and C, solid tumors, lupus, drugs, etc.

268
00:10:31,220 --> 00:10:32,420
Now in membranous nephropathy,

269
00:10:32,420 --> 00:10:35,840
the key finding is actually given away by the name membranous.

270
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:37,420
So what's going to happen is you're going to get

271
00:10:37,420 --> 00:10:40,240
thick glomerular basement membranes on H and E.

272
00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:44,200
So that filtration barrier that we normally would see on H and E

273
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:46,680
to be thin and delicate is going to become thick.

274
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:48,440
And so let me show you an image of that

275
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:49,840
just to highlight this principle.

276
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:51,340
Now here is a glomerulus.

277
00:10:51,340 --> 00:10:52,600
And when you look at this glomerulus,

278
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:56,100
what you can recognize is that the glomerular filtration membrane

279
00:10:56,100 --> 00:10:58,280
has become particularly thick.

280
00:10:58,280 --> 00:11:01,540
And so hence, we give it the name membranous nephropathy

281
00:11:01,540 --> 00:11:03,880
because that membrane has become thick.

282
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:06,180
Now, you can ask the question,

283
00:11:06,320 --> 00:11:07,760
why did the membrane become thick?

284
00:11:07,760 --> 00:11:10,360
And in the disorder membranous nephropathy,

285
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:13,960
the key problem is that you have immune complex deposition.

286
00:11:13,960 --> 00:11:15,200
I'll give you a little bit of a hint.

287
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:18,760
Any disease that has the word membranous in it,

288
00:11:18,760 --> 00:11:22,220
and there's only two, membranous and membranoproliferative,

289
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:23,800
which is going to be the next disorder

290
00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:25,000
that we're going to cover,

291
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,320
in all of those cases, you get thickening of the membrane,

292
00:11:28,320 --> 00:11:29,760
and the thickening of the membrane

293
00:11:29,760 --> 00:11:32,300
is due to immune complex deposition.

294
00:11:32,300 --> 00:11:34,060
All right, so that's going to be a general theme.

295
00:11:34,060 --> 00:11:37,160
And I'll highlight that one more time as we go along.

296
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:38,400
Now, in this particular case,

297
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:40,360
once you have immune complex deposition,

298
00:11:40,360 --> 00:11:42,820
then you should know that the immunofluorescence

299
00:11:42,820 --> 00:11:44,460
is going to be granular.

300
00:11:44,460 --> 00:11:47,700
This is important to highlight, so let me illustrate it.

301
00:11:47,700 --> 00:11:50,300
Here again, you've got the endothelial cell,

302
00:11:50,300 --> 00:11:53,200
and the endothelial cell sits on the basement membrane,

303
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:55,400
and upon that sits the podocyte.

304
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:57,520
Now, what's going to happen in this disorder

305
00:11:57,520 --> 00:12:01,560
is that we are going to get immune complex deposition

306
00:12:01,560 --> 00:12:04,900
at the level of the podocyte.

307
00:12:04,900 --> 00:12:07,020
So it will be right underneath the podocyte,

308
00:12:07,020 --> 00:12:09,500
or right under the epithelial cell,

309
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:12,160
and so it's also called subepithelial.

310
00:12:12,160 --> 00:12:13,760
Now, a few important things.

311
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:16,120
Number one, these deposits will be detectable

312
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:17,600
by immunofluorescence.

313
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:19,500
And the immunofluorescence is going to light up

314
00:12:19,500 --> 00:12:21,460
the little grains of deposit,

315
00:12:21,460 --> 00:12:23,400
or the little circular deposits,

316
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:26,300
which basically is then going to produce a granular,

317
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:29,040
or a grainy immunofluorescence.

318
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:30,900
At the same time, the other point to note

319
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:33,400
is that when these deposits are sitting

320
00:12:33,400 --> 00:12:36,640
underneath the epithelial cell, or the podocyte,

321
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:40,100
the podocyte will respond by trying to lay down

322
00:12:40,100 --> 00:12:41,600
additional basement membrane.

323
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:44,600
Basically, epithelial cells need to sit on basement membrane,

324
00:12:44,600 --> 00:12:46,160
and they don't like to be kicked off

325
00:12:46,160 --> 00:12:48,600
of their basement membrane by these deposits.

326
00:12:48,600 --> 00:12:51,040
Now, as they lay down additional basement membrane,

327
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:52,800
what's going to happen is you're going to generate

328
00:12:52,800 --> 00:12:55,560
a dome over the deposit.

329
00:12:55,560 --> 00:12:58,320
And then, let's pretend there's another deposit here.

330
00:12:58,320 --> 00:13:01,500
You're going to generate another dome over that deposit.

331
00:13:01,500 --> 00:13:04,600
And in between the dome, you're going to create these spikes.

332
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:07,260
And so you get this spike and dome,

333
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:10,860
spike and dome appearance on EM.

334
00:13:10,860 --> 00:13:13,860
And that's a classic description of the EM

335
00:13:13,860 --> 00:13:15,700
of membranous nephropathy.

336
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:18,120
Now, let's go back and just see what we say here in the notes.

337
00:13:18,120 --> 00:13:20,200
So membranous nephropathy is called membranous

338
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:23,660
because you get thick glomerular basement membranes on H and E.

339
00:13:23,660 --> 00:13:26,520
Of course, anytime you have the word membranous in the name

340
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:28,920
of the disorder when we're studying these disorders,

341
00:13:28,920 --> 00:13:31,760
it's going to be due to immune complex deposition.

342
00:13:31,760 --> 00:13:35,020
The immune complex deposition is going to give us a granular

343
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:38,160
immunofluorescence because we're going to light up each of those

344
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:40,760
little grainy immune complexes.

345
00:13:40,760 --> 00:13:43,760
Now, of course, because this is subepithelial and this disorder

346
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:46,420
is characterized by subepithelial deposits,

347
00:13:46,420 --> 00:13:49,080
we're going to generate a spike and dome appearance

348
00:13:49,080 --> 00:13:51,120
on the electron microscopy.

349
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:53,980
Here is the H and E image that I've already shown you.

350
00:13:53,980 --> 00:13:56,120
And the key problem here is that you've got thickening

351
00:13:56,120 --> 00:13:58,320
of this glomerular filtration barrier

352
00:13:58,320 --> 00:14:00,180
or thickening of the membrane.

353
00:14:00,180 --> 00:14:02,020
When you look at this on immunofluorescence,

354
00:14:02,020 --> 00:14:03,680
what you're going to see is this grainy

355
00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:05,720
or granular immunofluorescence.

356
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:07,180
And so just focus here for a minute.

357
00:14:07,180 --> 00:14:10,080
Can you appreciate all the little dots that are going,

358
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:11,880
that are being put together in order to create

359
00:14:11,880 --> 00:14:13,780
this immunofluorescence pattern?

360
00:14:13,780 --> 00:14:15,840
Again, that's the granular immunofluorescence.

361
00:14:15,840 --> 00:14:18,820
And granular immunofluorescence equals

362
00:14:18,820 --> 00:14:21,140
immune complex deposition problem.

363
00:14:21,140 --> 00:14:23,220
It tells you that there is immune complex

364
00:14:23,220 --> 00:14:25,120
depositing within that membrane.

365
00:14:25,120 --> 00:14:27,380
Of course, you would then do EM to try to localize

366
00:14:27,380 --> 00:14:28,980
where the immune complex is.

367
00:14:28,980 --> 00:14:30,980
And in this particular case, the immune complex

368
00:14:30,980 --> 00:14:33,820
is right underneath the podocyte.

369
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:35,740
And it's kicking off the podocyte.

370
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:37,980
Now, of course, when the podocyte is separated

371
00:14:37,980 --> 00:14:40,780
from its basement membrane, it's not going to be happy about that.

372
00:14:40,780 --> 00:14:43,520
And so it's going to lay down new basement membrane.

373
00:14:43,520 --> 00:14:45,180
And when it lays down new basement membrane,

374
00:14:45,180 --> 00:14:47,880
you're going to create these domes and then a spike

375
00:14:47,880 --> 00:14:50,540
and then another dome and then another dome.

376
00:14:50,540 --> 00:14:53,480
And this would be the spike here in between the deposits.

377
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:57,580
So again, domes on top of the deposits and spikes in between.

378
00:14:57,580 --> 00:15:00,120
The next nephrotic syndrome is membranoproliferative

379
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:01,480
glomerulonephritis.

380
00:15:01,480 --> 00:15:04,720
The term membrano tells you that you're going to get thickening

381
00:15:04,720 --> 00:15:07,020
of that capillary membrane on H and E.

382
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:08,920
And of course, whenever you have the word membrano

383
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:10,880
when you have thickening of the capillary membrane,

384
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:13,160
it's going to be due to immune complex deposition,

385
00:15:13,160 --> 00:15:14,380
as I've already explained.

386
00:15:14,380 --> 00:15:17,080
And of course, whenever you have immune complex deposition,

387
00:15:17,080 --> 00:15:19,420
you're going to have a granular immunofluorescence.

388
00:15:19,420 --> 00:15:22,720
Now, in addition, notice that we have the word proliferative here.

389
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:25,880
And what proliferative means is that the mesangial cell

390
00:15:25,880 --> 00:15:28,880
that's holding together all of those capillary loops,

391
00:15:28,880 --> 00:15:32,460
the cytoplasm of the mesangial cell is going to proliferate

392
00:15:32,460 --> 00:15:36,020
and it's going to, it's going to separate the deposit,

393
00:15:36,020 --> 00:15:38,660
creating a classic tram track appearance.

394
00:15:38,660 --> 00:15:39,820
Let's illustrate this.

395
00:15:39,820 --> 00:15:43,000
So here you have the endothelial cell.

396
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,020
And of course, the endothelial cell sits

397
00:15:45,020 --> 00:15:46,760
on top of basement membrane.

398
00:15:46,760 --> 00:15:50,420
And then above that, you've got the foot processes

399
00:15:50,420 --> 00:15:52,100
of the podocytes.

400
00:15:52,100 --> 00:15:53,620
Now in this particular disorder,

401
00:15:53,620 --> 00:15:57,300
let's imagine that you get a immune complex deposit.

402
00:15:57,300 --> 00:15:59,660
And the immune complex deposit in membranoproliferative

403
00:15:59,660 --> 00:16:02,460
can be underneath the endothelium or it can be

404
00:16:02,460 --> 00:16:03,900
in the basement membrane.

405
00:16:03,900 --> 00:16:06,020
Let's put it here underneath the endothelium

406
00:16:06,020 --> 00:16:07,260
for the time being.

407
00:16:07,260 --> 00:16:09,820
Now what's going to happen is here is the mesangial cell

408
00:16:09,820 --> 00:16:11,820
holding together the capillary.

409
00:16:11,820 --> 00:16:14,120
And when the mesangial cell sees this deposit,

410
00:16:14,120 --> 00:16:16,960
it will proliferate its cytoplasm and basically cut

411
00:16:16,960 --> 00:16:18,700
through the middle of that deposit.

412
00:16:18,700 --> 00:16:20,360
When it cuts through the middle of the deposit,

413
00:16:20,360 --> 00:16:24,320
it's going to create or separate the deposit into two pieces.

414
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:27,960
And these two pieces are going to look like a tram track.

415
00:16:27,960 --> 00:16:31,120
You know, two lines which basically form a track.

416
00:16:31,120 --> 00:16:33,100
And so that's why I say here in your notes

417
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:35,820
that it's going to have a classic tram track appearance.

418
00:16:35,820 --> 00:16:37,900
Now this is the easiest thing to remember

419
00:16:37,900 --> 00:16:39,260
for board examinations.

420
00:16:39,260 --> 00:16:40,900
Because the name tells you, right,

421
00:16:40,900 --> 00:16:42,460
that you've got a membrane,

422
00:16:42,460 --> 00:16:44,300
and that there's going to be proliferation

423
00:16:44,300 --> 00:16:46,820
of the mesangial cells, hence the word proliferative.

424
00:16:46,820 --> 00:16:49,460
And that proliferation of the cytoplasm of the mesangium

425
00:16:49,460 --> 00:16:51,500
is going to cut the deposit in half,

426
00:16:51,500 --> 00:16:53,760
creating two lines, which is a tram track.

427
00:16:53,760 --> 00:16:54,700
So it's a giveaway.

428
00:16:54,700 --> 00:16:56,460
If they were to ask you on the board exam,

429
00:16:56,460 --> 00:16:58,620
which of the following has a tram track appearance,

430
00:16:58,620 --> 00:17:01,700
it's a giveaway that the answer would be membranoproliferative.

431
00:17:01,700 --> 00:17:04,460
All right, so again, I use the name to help me

432
00:17:04,460 --> 00:17:06,100
because I think it takes you very far

433
00:17:06,100 --> 00:17:09,100
when you're considering many of the disorders in pathology.

434
00:17:09,100 --> 00:17:11,020
Now unfortunately, we're not done.

435
00:17:11,020 --> 00:17:14,460
When you have membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis,

436
00:17:14,460 --> 00:17:16,900
it's actually divided into two types.

437
00:17:16,900 --> 00:17:18,500
And it's divided into two types

438
00:17:18,500 --> 00:17:20,920
based on the location of the deposits.

439
00:17:20,920 --> 00:17:22,360
So let's illustrate this.

440
00:17:22,360 --> 00:17:24,320
This is the endothelial cell.

441
00:17:24,320 --> 00:17:26,360
That's sitting on top of a basement membrane.

442
00:17:26,360 --> 00:17:28,860
And of course, the podocytes are sitting on the opposite side.

443
00:17:28,860 --> 00:17:30,820
These are the epithelial cells.

444
00:17:30,820 --> 00:17:34,460
In type one membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis,

445
00:17:34,460 --> 00:17:38,700
you get deposits underneath the endothelial cell

446
00:17:38,700 --> 00:17:40,560
or subendothelial.

447
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:42,620
So this would be type one.

448
00:17:42,620 --> 00:17:44,960
And important to note that type one

449
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:48,020
is associated with hepatitis B virus

450
00:17:48,020 --> 00:17:51,480
and hepatitis C virus infections.

451
00:17:51,480 --> 00:17:53,380
That's particularly high yield.

452
00:17:53,380 --> 00:17:55,520
Now in type one, it's also important to note,

453
00:17:55,520 --> 00:17:57,880
and you should add this to the margin of your text,

454
00:17:57,880 --> 00:18:00,820
that type one is more often associated

455
00:18:00,820 --> 00:18:03,060
with the formation of tram tracts.

456
00:18:03,060 --> 00:18:05,260
Okay, so type one, I would say a few things.

457
00:18:05,260 --> 00:18:08,360
Number one, hepatitis B virus, hepatitis C virus,

458
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:09,980
and more often associated

459
00:18:09,980 --> 00:18:12,260
with the formation of tram tracts.

460
00:18:12,260 --> 00:18:16,020
In type two membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis,

461
00:18:16,020 --> 00:18:20,120
you get deposition of the immune complexes

462
00:18:20,120 --> 00:18:22,180
within the basement membrane.

463
00:18:22,180 --> 00:18:24,660
So this is type two.

464
00:18:24,660 --> 00:18:25,980
Now in type two,

465
00:18:25,980 --> 00:18:29,460
there is a very super high yield association.

466
00:18:29,460 --> 00:18:32,960
And that is that these patients have an antibody

467
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:36,420
in their blood called C3 nephritic factor.

468
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:38,260
Basically it's an autoantibody.

469
00:18:38,260 --> 00:18:40,280
It's called C3 nephritic factor,

470
00:18:40,280 --> 00:18:43,220
and it stabilizes C3 convertase.

471
00:18:43,220 --> 00:18:45,260
Now the stabilization of C3 convertase

472
00:18:45,260 --> 00:18:47,660
leads to the over-activation of complement,

473
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:49,360
which then results in inflammation,

474
00:18:49,360 --> 00:18:51,700
and then can drive this process forward.

475
00:18:51,700 --> 00:18:52,860
Now let me just hit home on this

476
00:18:52,860 --> 00:18:54,800
in order to highlight this high yield point.

477
00:18:54,800 --> 00:18:57,900
So remember that C3 is an important component of complement,

478
00:18:57,900 --> 00:19:03,560
and C3 gets converted into C3A and C3B.

479
00:19:03,560 --> 00:19:05,920
Now the enzyme that's going to cause this conversion

480
00:19:05,920 --> 00:19:09,360
is called C3 convertase.

481
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:12,100
Remember that whenever you generate C3 convertase,

482
00:19:12,100 --> 00:19:14,700
you very quickly destroy C3 convertase,

483
00:19:14,700 --> 00:19:17,100
because you only want to turn complement on

484
00:19:17,100 --> 00:19:20,260
for just a very, very limited period of time.

485
00:19:20,260 --> 00:19:23,140
So normally C3 convertase would be destroyed rapidly.

486
00:19:23,140 --> 00:19:25,200
However, in these patients with type two,

487
00:19:25,200 --> 00:19:27,500
membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis,

488
00:19:27,500 --> 00:19:30,860
they actually have an antibody that blocks C3 convertase

489
00:19:30,860 --> 00:19:32,340
and stabilizes it.

490
00:19:32,340 --> 00:19:33,660
Now it doesn't block the activity,

491
00:19:33,660 --> 00:19:36,500
but it stabilizes it so it can't be broken down.

492
00:19:36,500 --> 00:19:37,900
So when it can't be broken down,

493
00:19:37,900 --> 00:19:40,700
this is going to result in over-activation of complement,

494
00:19:40,700 --> 00:19:43,160
and so you're going to get a decreased serum C3

495
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:45,260
with an over-activation of complement.

496
00:19:45,260 --> 00:19:47,100
That's going to result in inflammation

497
00:19:47,100 --> 00:19:50,800
and inflammatory damage within the glomerulus.

498
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:51,600
Now with that in mind,

499
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:53,600
I'd like to highlight one more important point,

500
00:19:53,600 --> 00:19:56,100
and that is membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis

501
00:19:56,100 --> 00:19:58,340
can actually produce nephritic syndrome

502
00:19:58,340 --> 00:20:00,900
or nephrotic syndrome or both.

503
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:02,540
Now I put it in this discussion here

504
00:20:02,540 --> 00:20:05,440
because it fits well with the concept of membranous

505
00:20:05,440 --> 00:20:08,280
and then thinking about membranoproliferative after that.

506
00:20:08,280 --> 00:20:10,800
So I combined it here because it has the potential

507
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:12,480
to create nephrotic syndrome.

508
00:20:12,480 --> 00:20:13,640
However, you should bear in mind

509
00:20:13,640 --> 00:20:16,200
that it can also create nephritic syndrome,

510
00:20:16,200 --> 00:20:18,100
or sometimes it can create both.

511
00:20:18,100 --> 00:20:21,100
And of course, if you have over-activation of complement

512
00:20:21,100 --> 00:20:22,700
as you would have in type two,

513
00:20:22,700 --> 00:20:24,940
you're going to get inflammation within the glomerulus,

514
00:20:24,940 --> 00:20:26,480
and that inflammation is going to be more

515
00:20:26,480 --> 00:20:29,480
of a nephritic syndrome than it would be a nephrotic syndrome.

516
00:20:29,480 --> 00:20:31,580
However, for the purposes of learning,

517
00:20:31,580 --> 00:20:35,240
I think it's nice to put membranous glomerulonephropathy

518
00:20:35,240 --> 00:20:38,900
sort of right before membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis.

519
00:20:38,900 --> 00:20:40,400
Now another reason why I do it

520
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:42,280
is because it helps me to remember

521
00:20:42,280 --> 00:20:44,280
that in the membranotype diseases,

522
00:20:44,280 --> 00:20:46,640
whether it be membranous or membranoproliferative,

523
00:20:46,640 --> 00:20:49,600
you're always going to have immune complex deposits,

524
00:20:49,600 --> 00:20:51,900
and that those immune complex deposits

525
00:20:51,900 --> 00:20:53,640
will be in one of three locations.

526
00:20:53,640 --> 00:20:55,180
And the diseases together,

527
00:20:55,180 --> 00:20:57,200
they cover every possible location.

528
00:20:57,200 --> 00:21:00,140
What I mean by that is that here's the endothelial cell,

529
00:21:00,140 --> 00:21:01,880
and then you've got the basement membrane,

530
00:21:01,880 --> 00:21:05,280
and then on top of that you've got the podocyte, correct?

531
00:21:05,280 --> 00:21:07,820
Now what can happen here is that you can get deposits

532
00:21:07,820 --> 00:21:10,920
underneath the epithelium, that's called subepithelium,

533
00:21:10,920 --> 00:21:15,320
and that's seen in membranous glomerulonephropathy.

534
00:21:15,320 --> 00:21:17,980
And then you can also get deposits in this,

535
00:21:17,980 --> 00:21:22,180
underneath the endothelium, and that's seen in type one,

536
00:21:22,180 --> 00:21:25,140
membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis,

537
00:21:25,140 --> 00:21:27,780
or you can get deposits within the basement membrane,

538
00:21:27,780 --> 00:21:33,420
and that's seen in type two, membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis.

539
00:21:33,420 --> 00:21:36,520
So notice that in these membranous type diseases,

540
00:21:36,520 --> 00:21:38,340
there are, you covered all three layers, right?

541
00:21:38,340 --> 00:21:39,640
There was one possibility of being

542
00:21:39,640 --> 00:21:41,320
underneath the endothelial cell,

543
00:21:41,320 --> 00:21:43,640
one possibility of being in the basement membrane,

544
00:21:43,640 --> 00:21:46,480
and one possibility of being underneath the epithelial cell.

545
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:48,120
And so we covered all three.

546
00:21:48,120 --> 00:21:52,860
One, two, and then membranous up here.

547
00:21:52,860 --> 00:21:55,760
So all three were covered by these diseases.

548
00:21:55,760 --> 00:21:59,000
And again, in all of these diseases, the key problem is immune complex,

549
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,260
so you're gonna get a granular immunofluorescence, et cetera, et cetera.

550
00:22:02,260 --> 00:22:04,760
And so that's the way I like to think about these disorders.

551
00:22:04,760 --> 00:22:08,020
In fact, the way I overall think about nephrotic syndrome

552
00:22:08,020 --> 00:22:10,120
is that there's basically six causes,

553
00:22:10,120 --> 00:22:11,820
and I put them together in twos.

554
00:22:11,820 --> 00:22:14,620
So you've got minimal change disease and FSGS,

555
00:22:14,620 --> 00:22:18,960
and in both of those disorders, the key problem is effacement of the foot process,

556
00:22:18,960 --> 00:22:21,660
and then you've got membranous and membranoproliferative,

557
00:22:21,660 --> 00:22:26,160
and in those two disorders, the key problem is deposition of immune complexes,

558
00:22:26,160 --> 00:22:28,160
which is then going to give you granular immunofluorescence

559
00:22:28,160 --> 00:22:30,660
and all the classic findings that we've described.

560
00:22:30,660 --> 00:22:33,660
And then there's two more causes of nephrotic syndrome,

561
00:22:33,660 --> 00:22:34,660
so there's six in all.

562
00:22:34,660 --> 00:22:37,660
And the next two are going to be systemic diseases

563
00:22:37,660 --> 00:22:41,160
that then affect the glomerulus to cause a nephrotic syndrome.

564
00:22:41,160 --> 00:22:43,660
And so the first of those is diabetes mellitus.

565
00:22:43,660 --> 00:22:47,660
Now what happens in diabetes is that you get a high serum glucose, obviously.

566
00:22:47,660 --> 00:22:51,660
And the high serum glucose results in non-enzymatic glycosylation

567
00:22:51,660 --> 00:22:53,660
of the vascular basement membrane.

568
00:22:53,660 --> 00:22:56,660
Now when you stick sugar on the basement membrane

569
00:22:56,660 --> 00:23:00,660
without the use of enzyme, that's called non-enzymatic glycosylation.

570
00:23:00,660 --> 00:23:03,660
Of course, when that happens to the vascular basement membrane,

571
00:23:03,660 --> 00:23:06,660
that's going to result in the basement membrane becoming leaky,

572
00:23:06,660 --> 00:23:09,660
and so what's going to happen is that protein will leak into the wall

573
00:23:09,660 --> 00:23:13,660
of the blood vessel, creating something called highline arteriolosclerosis.

574
00:23:13,660 --> 00:23:17,660
And we talked about highline arteriolosclerosis in the vascular section,

575
00:23:17,660 --> 00:23:21,660
and recall there, I told you that highline arteriolosclerosis

576
00:23:21,660 --> 00:23:26,660
occurs with both hypertension, in particular benign hypertension, and diabetes.

577
00:23:26,660 --> 00:23:28,660
Now when you get highline arteriolosclerosis,

578
00:23:28,660 --> 00:23:31,660
the problem is that you get thickening of the blood vessel wall

579
00:23:31,660 --> 00:23:34,660
with this protein-like material,

580
00:23:34,660 --> 00:23:37,660
which then results in a decrease in the caliber of the lumen.

581
00:23:37,660 --> 00:23:40,660
Now it's important to note that in diabetes, what actually happens

582
00:23:40,660 --> 00:23:44,660
is that if this is the afferent arteriole, and here is the efferent arteriole,

583
00:23:44,660 --> 00:23:49,660
you get preferential highline arteriolosclerosis in the efferent arteriole.

584
00:23:49,660 --> 00:23:53,660
Now that's going to reduce the caliber of the lumen,

585
00:23:53,660 --> 00:23:56,660
and creating an increased pressure in the glomerulus, right?

586
00:23:56,660 --> 00:24:00,660
If you tighten this, there's going to be more back pressure here in the glomerulus.

587
00:24:00,660 --> 00:24:05,660
That increased pressure in the glomerulus is going to create hyperfiltration,

588
00:24:05,660 --> 00:24:09,660
which is going to initially begin as microalbuminuria,

589
00:24:09,660 --> 00:24:15,660
but eventually you'll get sclerosis of the mesangium of the glomerulus,

590
00:24:15,660 --> 00:24:18,660
and as you get sclerosis of the mesangium of the glomerulus,

591
00:24:18,660 --> 00:24:20,660
it'll progress to nephrotic syndrome.

592
00:24:20,660 --> 00:24:23,660
Now one of the important things to recognize concerning this pathogenesis

593
00:24:23,660 --> 00:24:26,660
is that if you give patients ACE inhibitors,

594
00:24:26,660 --> 00:24:29,660
you can actually slow the progression of the hyperfiltration injury.

595
00:24:29,660 --> 00:24:34,660
And the reason is because angiotensin II also squeezes down here,

596
00:24:34,660 --> 00:24:36,660
and so now that additional squeezing down

597
00:24:36,660 --> 00:24:39,660
is only going to create additional hyperfiltration injury.

598
00:24:39,660 --> 00:24:41,660
So if you can block angiotensin II,

599
00:24:41,660 --> 00:24:44,660
you'll decrease some of that pressure at the efferent arteriole,

600
00:24:44,660 --> 00:24:47,660
which will then decrease the hyperfiltration injury.

601
00:24:47,660 --> 00:24:49,660
So let's read together what we say in the text.

602
00:24:49,660 --> 00:24:51,660
In diabetes, you get high serum glucose

603
00:24:51,660 --> 00:24:55,660
that leads to non-enzymatic glicosylation of the vascular basement membrane.

604
00:24:55,660 --> 00:24:58,660
By the way, that's the very first change that occurs in the kidney.

605
00:24:58,660 --> 00:25:02,660
Non-enzymatic glicosylation of the vascular basement membrane.

606
00:25:02,660 --> 00:25:05,660
Sometimes examiners will ask you what's the first change.

607
00:25:05,660 --> 00:25:08,660
This then results in highline arteriolosclerosis.

608
00:25:08,660 --> 00:25:12,660
Of course, the efferent arteriole is preferentially affected,

609
00:25:12,660 --> 00:25:14,660
leading to a high glomerular filtration pressure.

610
00:25:14,660 --> 00:25:18,660
The high filtration pressure results in microalbuminuria.

611
00:25:18,660 --> 00:25:20,660
This eventually progresses to nephrotic syndrome.

612
00:25:20,660 --> 00:25:22,660
The hallmark of nephrotic syndrome would be

613
00:25:22,660 --> 00:25:24,660
that you would see sclerosis of the mesangium,

614
00:25:24,660 --> 00:25:28,660
and that sclerosis results in these classic nodules

615
00:25:28,660 --> 00:25:30,660
called Kimmel-Steele-Wilson nodules.

616
00:25:30,660 --> 00:25:33,660
And as I've mentioned, ACE inhibitors would slow the progression

617
00:25:33,660 --> 00:25:36,660
of hyperfiltration-induced damage.

618
00:25:36,660 --> 00:25:37,660
So here's a picture of the glomerulus.

619
00:25:37,660 --> 00:25:39,660
Here's the round glomerulus here.

620
00:25:39,660 --> 00:25:42,660
And notice that in the mesangium, you've got this dense sclerosis.

621
00:25:42,660 --> 00:25:45,660
And of course, these are Kimmel-Steele-Wilson nodules.

622
00:25:45,660 --> 00:25:47,660
There's a perfect one right here.

623
00:25:47,660 --> 00:25:50,660
Very high yield, as this is pathognomonic for diabetes.

624
00:25:50,660 --> 00:25:53,660
The last disorder that can create nephrotic syndrome

625
00:25:53,660 --> 00:25:55,660
would be systemic amyloidosis.

626
00:25:55,660 --> 00:25:57,660
Remember that when patients have systemic amyloidosis,

627
00:25:57,660 --> 00:26:00,660
the kidney is the most commonly involved organ.

628
00:26:00,660 --> 00:26:02,660
I told you that when we talked about amyloid.

629
00:26:02,660 --> 00:26:05,660
The amyloid will deposit in the mesangium,

630
00:26:05,660 --> 00:26:07,660
and that will result in a nephrotic syndrome.

631
00:26:07,660 --> 00:26:10,660
Of course, a biopsy would show Congo red staining

632
00:26:10,660 --> 00:26:14,660
with apple green birefringents under polarized light.

633
00:26:14,660 --> 00:26:16,660
Now basically, in summary, we've done the nephrotic syndromes,

634
00:26:16,660 --> 00:26:18,660
but just remember there are six nephrotic syndromes,

635
00:26:18,660 --> 00:26:20,660
and study them in pairs.

636
00:26:20,660 --> 00:26:22,660
Minimal change disease, FSGS.

637
00:26:22,660 --> 00:26:25,660
Membranous, membranoproliferative.

638
00:26:25,660 --> 00:26:27,660
And then diabetes, and amyloid.

639
00:26:27,660 --> 00:26:30,660
And so in each of these, again, you have some common themes.

640
00:26:30,660 --> 00:26:33,660
And I think if you understand nephrotic syndrome in this way,

641
00:26:33,660 --> 00:26:35,660
you'll be very strong in this topic.

642
00:26:35,660 --> 00:26:38,660
That concludes our discussion of nephrotic syndrome.

